<details><summary>Click to expand</summary>We will actually prove that it is impossible for  $AB,AC,BD,CE$  to have all integer lengths, which will of course prove the original result. Assume that we have a triangle with  $AB,AC,BD,CE$  all integers. Let  $\alpha=\angle ABD$  and  $\beta=\angle ACE$ . Then
		\begin{align*}
			\cos\alpha&=\frac{AB}{BD}=r,\text{ and} 
			\cos\beta&=\frac{AC}{CE}=s,
		\end{align*}
		for some rational numbers  $r$  and  $s$ . In addition, note that  $2\alpha+2\beta=90^\circ$ , so  $\alpha+\beta=45^\circ$ . Therefore we have
		\begin{align*}
			\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}=\cos 45^\circ&=\cos(\alpha+\beta) 
			&=\cos\alpha\cos\beta-\sin\alpha\sin\beta 
			&=rs-\sqrt{1-r^2}\sqrt{1-s^2} 
			&=rs-\sqrt{(1-r^2)(1-s^2)}
		\end{align*}
		This implies that  $\sqrt{(1-r^2)(1-s^2)}$  is irrational, since otherwise the left-hand side would be irrational while the right-hand side would be rational, a contradiction. However, if we square both sides, we obtain
		\begin{align*}
			\frac{1}{2}&=\left(rs-\sqrt{(1-r^2)(1-s^2)}\right)^2 
			&=r^2s^2+(1-r^2)(1-s^2)-2rs\sqrt{(1-r^2)(1-s^2)}
		\end{align*}
		From this equation, the left-hand side is rational, so the right-hand side must be rational as well. This means in particular that  $\sqrt{(1-r^2)(1-s^2)}$  must be rational. Therefore  $\sqrt{(1-r^2)(1-s^2)}$  is both rational and irrational, contradiction. $\hfill\blacksquare$</details>

