<details><summary>My Solution</summary>Let  $a=x-2010$ . Then the inequality reduces to   $$ \frac{1}{a+1}+\frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{a-1}\geq 1 $$   Taking a common denominator yields   $$ \frac{a(a-1)+(a+1)(a-1)+a(a+1)}{(a+1)(a-1)a}\geq 1 $$   Upon simplifying, we get   $$ \frac{3a^2-1}{a^3-a}\geq 1 $$   We have to be careful when we multiply both sides by  $a^3-a$  because that could potentially change the  $\geq$  to  $\leq$  depending on what  $a$  is. So we will just leave the possible  $\geq$  or  $\leq$  as  $?$ . Multiplying  $a^3-a$  on both sides yields  $3a^2-1 ? a^3-a\iff -a^3+3a^2+a-1 ? 0$ . We will draw a rough sketch of the graph of  $-a^3+3a^2+a-1$  to get a better view of things: 
[asy]
Label f; 
f.p=fontsize(3); 
xaxis(-20,20,Ticks(f, 2.0, 1.0)); 
yaxis(-20,20,Ticks(f, 2.0, 1.0));
real f(real x) 
{ 
return -x^3+3x^2+x-1; 
} 
draw(graph(f,-2,4));
[/asy]
Now let's focus on that  $?$ . We found that  $?=\geq$  if  $a(a-1)(a+1)\geq 0$ , and  $?=\leq$  if  $a(a-1)(a+1)\leq 0$ . 
Now we make a chart.
\begin{tabular}{c|c}
a(a-1)(a+1) & Sign   \hline
(-infinity, -1) & -    \hline
(-1, 0) & +   \hline
(0, 1) & -   \hline
(1, infinity) & +   \hline
\end{tabular}
So now we will color the region the graph must be in in order for the inequality to work:
[asy]
Label f; 
f.p=fontsize(3); 
xaxis(-20,20,Ticks(f, 2.0, 1.0)); 
yaxis(-20,20,Ticks(f, 2.0, 1.0));
{
fill((-20, 0)--(-1, 0)--(-1, -20)--(-20, -20)--cycle, lightgreen);
fill((-1, 0)--(0, 0)--(0, 20)--(-1, 20)--cycle, lightgreen);
fill((0,0)--(1,0)--(1, -20)--(0,-20)--cycle, lightgreen);
fill((1,0)--(20,0)--(20,20)--(1,20)--cycle, lightgreen);
}
real f(real x) 
{ 
return -x^3+3x^2+x-1; 
} 
draw(graph(f,-2,4));

[/asy]
Let  $x_1, x_2, x_3$  be the roots of our equation, with  $x_1<x_2<x_3$ .
Now let's count the intervals. 

For the first part, or the interval  $(-20, -1)$ , there is no intersection with the green region.
For the second part, or the interval  $(-1, 0)$ , the intersection goes from  $-1$  to  $x_1$ , so the length of that interval is  $x_1+1$ .
For the third part, or the interval  $(0, 1)$ , the intersection goes from  $0$  to  $x_2$ , so the length of that interval is  $x_2$ .
For the last part, or the interval  $(1, 20)$ , the intersection goes from  $1$  to  $x_3$ , so the length of that interval  $x_3-1$ .
All in all, the sum of the lengths of the interval is  $x_1+1+x_2+x_3-1=x_1+x_2+x_3$ , adn by Vieta's formula's, this is equal to  $\frac{(-1)^1\cdot3}{-1}=\boxed{\textbf{(C) } 3}$ .</details>

