Suppose there are  $w$  women. We place the men into non-adjacent groups. We think of the groups of men as being inserted between women, or at the front or end of the lines, giving us a total of  $w+1$  slots to place the groups. In order for the conditions of the problem to be satisfied, we must have either 3 non-adjacent groups of 2 men, in which case there are  $\binom{w+1}{3}$  ways to place the men; 2 non-adajcent groups of 3 men, in which case there are  $\binom{w+1}{2}$  ways to place them; 2 non-adjacent groups in which one group contains 4 men and the other contains 2, in which case there are  $2\binom{w+1}{2}$  ways to place them, or one group of 6 men, in which case there are  $\binom{w+1}{1}$  ways to place them. Hence, the probability there are some 4 men standing together is
\begin{align*}
\frac{\binom{w+1}{1} + 2 \binom{w+1}{2}}{\binom{w+1}{1} + 2 \binom{w+1}{2} + \binom{w+1}{2} + \binom{w+1}{3}}
&= \frac{(w+1) + w(w+1)}{(w+1) + \frac{3w(w+1)}{2} + \frac{(w+1)(w)(w-1)}{6}} 
&= \frac{1 + w}{1 + \frac{3w}{2} + \frac{w(w-1)}{6}} 
&= \frac{6 + 6w}{6 + 8w + w^2}.
\end{align*}

In order for the probability to be less than 0.01, we must have  $\frac{6 + 6w}{6 + 8w + w^2} < 0.01$ , or  $600 + 600w < 6 + 8w + w^2$ , or  $w > 592 + \frac{594}{w}$ . It is easy to see that the smallest  $w$  satisfying this is  $w=\boxed{594}$ .

Begin by noting that two roots have the same sign, without loss of generality let them be  $a$  and  $b$ . Moreover we can assume that they are positive under the transformation  $(a, b, c) \mapsto (-a, -b, -c)$  which preserves the polynomial. Then we have,
\begin{align*}
a + b + c &= 0
ab + bc + ca &= -2011
\end{align*}
Substitute  $c = - a - b$  to find,
\begin{align*}
a^2 + ab + b^2 &= 2011
\end{align*}
whence we assume without loss of generality that  $a \leq b$  to find  $3a^2 \leq 2011 \iff a \leq 25$ . However from the quadratic formula we also know that,
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{8044 - 3a^2} \in \mathbb{Z} 
\end{align*}
Noting that  $8044 \approx 89^2$  we test values for  $m \leq 89$  such that  $\frac{8044 - m^2}{3}$  is a perfect square. Luckily we find at  $m = 88$  the quantity is  $100$  so  $a = 10$ . From here the finish is easy, yielding  $(a, b, c) = (10, 39, -49)$  for an answer of  $\boxed{098}$ .

