Here's a solution that I believe uses the least computation of those posted:

Let  $f(z)$  be the given polynomial. Note that if  $z$  is a zero  $f(z)$ , then  $z^{12} = 2^{36} = 8^{12}.$  From here it is well known that if we plot the twelve zeroes of  $f$  in the complex plane, then they are equally spaced on a circle centered at the origin. In addition, since  $z^{12} = 8^{12}$ , it follows that each complex number on this circle has magnitude  $8$ , so the circle has radius  $8.$  Since the  $12$  points  $z_1, z_2, ..., z_{12}$  are equally spaced, we deduce that the angle between each pair of consecutive points is  $30^{\circ} = \frac{\pi}{6}.$  Thus, each zero of  $f$  has a magnitude of  $8$  and an argument that is a multiple of  $\frac{\pi}{6}$ , so each zero  $z_j$  takes on coordinates of the form  $z_j = 8e^{ki\frac{\pi}{6}}$  where  $k \in \{1, 2, ..., 12\}.$  Now, I would attach a diagram, but you can basically look at a clock and see the same picture :P. 

We wish to find out when we want to keep a zero  $z_j$  and when we would rather replace it with  $iz_j.$  In other words, when is the real part of  $z_j$  larger than the real part of  $iz_j$  and vice versa.

Note that since  $i = e^{i\frac{\pi}{2}}$ , multiplying a complex number  $z$  by  $i$  is equivalent to rotating  $z$  counterclockwise by  $\frac{\pi}{2}$  radians about the origin. Therefore, if we let  $z_1 = e^{i\frac{\pi}{6}}, z_2 = e^{2i\frac{\pi}{6}}, ..., z_{12} = e^{12i\frac{\pi}{6}}$ , then we see that \[iz_j = \left(e^{i\frac{\pi}{2}}\right)\left(e^{ji\frac{\pi}{6}}\right) = e^{(j + 3)i\frac{\pi}{6}} = z_{j + 3}\] which is just the point  $z_j$  rotated by  $\frac{\pi}{2}$  radians about the origin. Therefore, we only need to figure out whether the real part of  $z_j$  or  $z_{j + 3}$  is larger, which is easy! By looking at our picture (a clock), it's easy to see that we want to keep  $z_1$  through  $z_4$ , since  $\Re(z_1) > \Re(iz_1) = \Re(z_4), \Re(z_2) > \Re(iz_2) = \Re(z_5)$ , etc, where  $\Re(w)$  denotes the real part of a complex number  $w.$  However, by consulting our diagram again, we see that for  $j \in \{5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10\}$ , the real part of  $z_j$  is less than the real part of  $iz_j = z_{j + 3}.$  Therefore, for  $j \in \{5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10\}$ , we will swap  $z_j$  for  $iz_j = z_{j + 3}.$  Finally, for  $j \in \{11, 12\}$ , it is easy to see that we wish to keep  $z_j.$  

By adding up which zeroes  $z_j$  we decided to keep and which ones we swapped for  $iz_j$ , it follows that the maximum value of  $\sum\limits_{j = 1}^{12} w_j$  is \[\Re(z_8) + \Re(z_9) + \Re(z_{10}) + 2\Re(z_{11}) + 2\Re(z_{12}) + 2\Re(z_1) + \Re(z_2) + \Re(z_3) + \Re(z_4).\] But by consulting our diagram once again, it is easy to see that  $z_2$  and  $z_4$  are simply reflections of each other about the imaginary axis, so  $\Re(z_2) + \Re(z_4) = 0.$  Similarly, we see that  $\Re(z_8) + \Re(z_{10}) = 0.$  Lastly, it is obvious that  $\Re(z_3) = \Re(z_9) = 0.$  Putting all of this together, we wish to compute 
\begin{align*}
&\quad \; 2\Re(z_{11}) + 2\Re(z_{12}) + 2\Re(z_1) 
&= 2\Re(z_{12}) + 4\Re(z_1) 
&= 2(8) + 4(8)\left(\cos 30^{\circ}\right) 
&= 16 + 16\sqrt{3} 
&= 16 + \sqrt{768}
\end{align*}
which gives us a final answer of  $16 + 768 = \boxed{784}.$

