Suppose there are   $x$  zeros,  $y$  ones and  $z$  twos modulo  $3$ . We have to solve the system   $$ xy+\dbinom{z}{2}=\dfrac13\dbinom{2011}{2} $$    $$ yz+\dbinom{x}{2}=\dfrac13\dbinom{2011}{2} $$    $$ zx+\dbinom{y}{2}=\dfrac13\dbinom{2011}{2} $$  First assume that  $x,y,z$  are all distinct. Then,   $$ xy+\dbinom{z}{2}=yz+\dbinom{x}{2} $$     $$ \Rightarrow y(x-z)=\dfrac{(x-z)(x+z-1)}{2} $$     $$ \Rightarrow y=\dfrac{z+x-1}{2}\qquad	ext{since x}
eq	ext{z} $$  Similarly,   $$ z=\dfrac{x+y-1}{2} $$     $$ x=\dfrac{y+z-1}{2} $$   Adding the three equations, we get   $$ 2x+2y+2z=2x+2y+2z-3 $$   which is an obvious contradiction. Hence, at least two of  $x,y,z$  must be equal. If all of them are equal, then  $3x=x+y+z=2011$  which is impossible since  $3$  does not divide  $2011$ . So, WLOG,  $x=y
eq z$ . Then, once again,   $$  egin{aligned} y=\dfrac{z+x-1}{2}\qquad 	ext{since x}
eq	ext{z} & \Rightarrow y=\dfrac{z+y-1}{2}\end{aligned} $$   Now, this combined with  $x+y+z=2x+z=2011$  gives us the solution  $(x,y,z)=(670,670,671)$ . It remains to check that these values do satisfy the equations above (checking is necessary since we never used the fact that the right side of each equation had a  $\dbinom{2011}{2}$  ). The other solutions are merely  permutations of this triple. 

On reflection, I think it should have been obvious that if a triple existed,  $x$ ,  $y$  and  $z$  should be as close to each other as possible. I guess I was so obsessed with disproving the existence of such triples  that I didn't try this approach  even though it had entered my mind back then.

