 $\boxed{\text{Yes}}.$  This problem is equivalent to finding a  $n$  such that there are at least two distinct solutions to   $$ 2\binom{n}{x}=\binom{n}{y}. $$  Let  $x<y<\frac{n}{2}.$  We consider the cases of when  $y=x+1$  and  $y=x+2.$  Note that  $y=x+1$  has a working value of  $y$  when  $n\equiv 2 \pmod 3.$  Now, for  $y=x+2.$  
This case is isomorphic to solving   $$ \frac{m+k}{x}\cdot \frac{m+k+1}{m+1}=2 $$   where  $m=x+1.$  
Expanding, we get   $$ m^2+(1-2k)m-(k^2+k)=0, $$  so we get that   $$ m=\frac{2k-1 \pm \sqrt{8k^2+1}}{2}. $$  Using Pell on  $8k^2+1,$  we get that our first working solution is  $(m,k)=(84,35),$  which means  $(x,y)=(83,85)$  and  $n=2m+k=203\equiv 2 \pmod 3.$  
Thus our binomials are 
\begin{align*}
2\binom{203}{83} &= 2\binom{203}{85} 
2\binom{203}{67} &= 2\binom{203}{68}.\blacksquare 
\end{align*}

