[/asy]

Let  $L_k$  be the number of black cells in the  $k$ th layer, which is the ring of cells on the perimeter of a  $2k + 1 \times 2k + 1$  square centered at the center of  $Q$ . Additionally, let  $X_k$  be the number of black squares in the  $k$ th layer along the diagonals, and let  $B = \sum L_i$  be the total number of black cells.



Now consider the set  $S_k$  of all  $2 \times 2$  squares which contain cells from layers  $k$  and  $k - 1$ . There are  $8k - 4$  such squares, one associated with each non diagonal cell of layer  $k$ . Consider the sum:
\[ R_k = \sum_ {s \in S_k} (\text{ \# of black squares in  $S$ }) \]
 As each term of this sum is  $\le 2$ , this sum is  $\le 2(8n - 4)$ . Considering each type of square(see diagram), we see: 



- Each of the  $X_k$  diagonal black cells in layer  $k$  is counted  $1$  time.
- Each of the  $L_k - X_k$  non-diagonal black cells in layer  $k$  is counted  $2$  times.
- Each of the  $X_{k - 1}$  diagonal black cells in layer  $k$  is counted  $3$  times.
- Each of the  $L_{k - 1} - X_{k - 1}$  non-diagonal black cells in layer  $k$  is counted  $2$  times.


 Therefore,
 \[ R_k = X_k + 2(L_k - X_k) + 3X_{k - 1} + 2(L_{k - 1} - X_{k - 1}) = 2L_k + 2L_{k - 1} + (X_{k - 1} - X_k) \le 2(8n - 4) .\]
Using the above, we get
 \[ L_k + L_{k - 1} = R_k - \frac{X_{k - 1} - X_k}2 \le 8n - 4 - \frac{X_{k - 1} - X_k}{2} \le 8n - 4 - \frac{0 - 4}{2} = 8n - 2 . \]
 where we have used that by definition,  $0 \le X_i \le 4$ .

Adding up this inequality repeatedly along with  $L_0 \le 1$ (if  $n$  is even) gives us the desired upper bound on  $L_0 + L_1 + \dots + L_n = B$ .

I claim that the maximum is   $$ (n+1)(2n+1)=\boxed{2n^2+3n+1}, $$   which is achievable by coloring  $n+1$  non-adjacent rows all black. I proceed with induction. 

For the base case, it is obvious that at most  $6$  black squares can be colored black in a  $3 \times 3$  grid, as desired. 

Then, suppose that at most   $$ (2k+1)(k+1)=2k^2+3k+1 $$   squares can be colored black in a  $(2k+1) \times (2k+1)$  board for some positive integer  $k.$  Let us consider the L-shape formed by taking a  $(2k+1) \times (2k+1)$  square out of the corner of a  $(2k+3) \times (2k+3)$  square. We can split this into  $2k+1$   $2 \times 2$  squares and a small L-shaped tetromino, as shown: 

[asy]
size(200);
pen gridColor = black + 0.75;
pen redFill = red;
pen greenFill = green;
pen boldBorder = black + 1.5;

for (int i = 0; i <= 9; ++i) {
  draw((i,0)--(i,9), gridColor);
  draw((0,i)--(9,i), gridColor);
}

void shade(pair p, pen color) {
  fill((p.x-1, p.y-1)--(p.x, p.y-1)--(p.x, p.y)--(p.x-1, p.y)--cycle, color);
}

void outlineRect(int x, int y, int w, int h) {
  draw((x-1, y-1)--(x-1+w, y-1)--(x-1+w, y-1+h)--(x-1, y-1+h)--cycle, boldBorder);
}

outlineRect(1, 1, 7, 7);

for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k) {
  int y = 1 + 2*k;
  pair[] block = {(8,y), (9,y), (9,y+1), (8,y+1)};
  for (pair p : block) shade(p, redFill);
  outlineRect(8, y, 2, 2);
}

for (int k = 0; k < 4; ++k) {
  int x = 1 + 2*k;
  pair[] block = {(x,8), (x,9), (x+1,9), (x+1,8)};
  for (pair p : block) shade(p, redFill);
  outlineRect(x, 8, 2, 2);
}

pair[] tetromino = {(8,7), (9,7), (9,8), (9,9)};
for (pair p : tetromino) shade(p, greenFill);
path L = (7,6)--(9,6)--(9,9)--(8,9)--(8,7)--(7,7)--cycle;
draw(L, boldBorder);
[/asy]

It is not difficult to see that we cannot color all of the squares in the tetromino black, so the maximum number of black squares that can be colored in the L-shape is
\begin{align*}
    (2k+1) \cdot 2 + 3 &= 4k+2+3 
    &= 4k+5.
\end{align*}
It results that the maximum number of black squares that can be colored in the whole  $(2k+3) \times (2k+3)$  grid is
\begin{align*}
    2k^2+3k+1+4k+5 &= 2k^2+7k+6 
    &= (2k+3)(k+2),
\end{align*}
concluding the inductive step. 

Hence, by the Principle of Mathematical Induction, the maximum amount of black cells that a  $(2n+1) \times (2n+1)$  board may have is  $2n^2+3n+1,$  as claimed.  $\square$

We claim the answer is  $2n^2+3n+1$ .

We start with a construction. Consider coloring every odd row completely black, and every even row completely white. This gives \[(n+1)(2n+1) = 2n^2+3n+1\] black squares, and clearly works, so we are done.  $\square$ We now provide a bound using induction. Clearly, if  $n = 1$ , then  $2\cdot 1^2+3\cdot 1^2+1\cdot 1^2 = 6$  is achievable with the configuration:
[asy]
size(4cm);
pair[] blackSquares = {(0,0), (0,1), (0,2), (2,0), (2,1), (2,2)};

for (pair p : blackSquares) {
  fill((p.x, p.y)--(p.x+1, p.y)--(p.x+1, p.y+1)--(p.x, p.y+1)--cycle, gray);
}

for (int i = 0; i <= 3; ++i) {
  draw((0,i)--(3,i), black);
  draw((i,0)--(i,3), black);
}
[/asy]
which is clearly the maximum. Now, assume that the maximum for  $n-1$  is  $2(n-1)^2+3(n-1)+1 = 2n^2-n$ . Then, consider splitting the grid into a  $2 \times 2$ , two  $2 \times 2n-1$ , and a  $2n-1 \times 2n-1$ , in the following way:
[asy]
size(6cm);

for (int i = 0; i <= 7; ++i) {
  draw((0,i)--(7,i), black);
  draw((i,0)--(i,7), black);
}

draw((0,0)--(0,2)--(2,2)--(2,0)--cycle, red+2);
draw((2,2)--(2,0)--(7,0)--(7,2)--cycle, red+2);
draw((2,2)--(0,2)--(0,7)--(2,7)--cycle, red+2);
draw((2,2)--(2,7)--(7,7)--(7,2)--cycle, red+2);
[/asy]
Clearly, the  $2 \times 2$  tile has a maximum of  $2$  black squares. In addition, at most one of the  $2 \times 2n-1$  can have  $2n$  tiles, which is the maximum for its tile size (can be proven easily), while the other tile will have a maximum of  $2n-1$  due to the  $2 \times 2$  tile. Finally, by the inductive hypothesis, the  $2n-1 \times 2n-1$  square has a maximum of  $2n^2-n$ . Hence, in total, the maximum is \[2n^2-n+2n+2n-1+2 = 2n^2+3n+1\] as required.  $\square$

