<details><summary>Nothing to see here, move on</summary>If  $a_{n}<a_{n-1}$  for some  $n$ , then  $a_{n+1}=\left(2-\frac 1N\right)a_n-a_{n-1}<2a_n-a_{n-1}<a_n$ . So if the sequence  $\langle a_i\rangle$  decreases at some point, it decreases ever after. Contrapositively, if  $\langle a_i\rangle$  is increasing upto some point, then it must have been increasing ever before.

Also, we notice that the given equation may be written as  $(a_{n+1}-a_n)=(a_n-a_{n-1})-\frac{a_n}{N}$ , so applying this over and over again, we have 

\begin{align*} a_{n+1}-a_n=& (a_n-a_{n-1})-\frac{a_n}{N}
=&(a_{n-1}-a_{n-2})-\frac{a_{n-1}}{N}-\frac{a_n}{N}
\vdots &
=&(a_1-a_0)-\frac{a_1+a_2+\cdots +a_n}{N}
\implies a_{n+1}=&1+a_n-\frac{a_1+a_2+\cdots +a_n}{N} & (1)
=&1+\frac{(a_n-a_1)+(a_n-a_2)+\cdots +(a_n-a_n)}{N} & (2)\end{align*}

If  $\langle a_i\rangle$  is increasing all the way upto  $a_N$ , i.e., if  $a_N\ge a_{N-1}\ge\cdots\ge a_1=1$ , then setting  $n=N$  in  $(1)$  gives   $$  a_{N+1}-a_N=1-\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{N}a_i}{N}< 1-\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{N}a_1}{N}=1-\frac{N}{N}=0. $$  So  $\langle a_i\rangle$  decreases past that point. This means we only need to worry about  $n\le N$ .

Now let us induct on  $n$ . The base case is trivial. Assume  $a_1,a_2,\cdots ,a_n<\sqrt{N+1}$ . If  $a_{n+1}\le a_n$ , then we were immediately done. Otherwise, if  $a_{n+1}>a_n$ , then the first paragraph implies  $a_1<a_2<\cdots <a_n$ . So from  $(2)$ , 
\begin{align*} a_{n+1}=& 1+\frac{(a_n-a_1)+(a_n-a_2)+\cdots +(a_n-a_n)}{N}
<& 1+\frac{(a_n-a_1)+(a_n-a_1)+\cdots +(a_n-a_1)}{N}
<& 1+\frac{n\left(\sqrt{N+1}-1\right)}{N}\le 1+(\sqrt{N+1}-1) \quad\text{[Because }n\le N]
=&\sqrt{N+1}\end{align*}

This completes our induction, and hence also the proof.  $\blacksquare$</details>

