We can first create a picture to help us understand the problem more
[asy]
draw((-2,0)--(-2,2)--(0,0)--cycle);
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--cycle);
fill((-2,2)--(-1.75,2.25)--(-1.5,2)--(-1.75,1.75)--cycle);
draw((1.5,0)--(1.5,1.5),dotted);
draw((-1.53,1.78)--(-1,1.25), red, arrow=Arrow(4));
label(' $\mu_k$ ', (1,1.5));
label(' $h_1$ ', (-2.25,1));
label(' $h_2$ ', (1.25,0.75));
[/asy]
We know by conservation of energy we can create the equation  $$ mgh_1=mgh_2+\mu_k mg\cos\theta\cdot\frac{h_2}{\sin\theta} $$  If you are confused by this equation, let us emphasize more on conservation of energy. The energy of the block at the top of the first ramp is simply  $mgh_1$ , by conservation of energy we must find the enrgy the block will display with both frictional and potential forces. The energy when the block is at height  $h_2$  on the second ramp is simply  $mgh_2$ . Lastly we must find the energy lost by the frictional force, since the formula for energy lost by the frictional force is $\mu_k F_N L$  and we know that  $F_N=mg\cos\theta$  all we have to do is find  $L$ . Which is  $\frac{h_2}{\sin\theta}$  by trigonometry, thus we get the equation  $$ mgh_1=mgh_2+\mu_k mg\cos\theta\cdot\frac{h_2}{\sin\theta} $$  taking away the  $mg$ ,   $$ h_1=h_2+\mu_k\cos\theta\cdot\frac{h_2}{\sin\theta} $$  thus,  $h_2=\frac{h_1\sin\theta}{\mu_k\cos\theta+\sin\theta}$  plugging in the respective values we obtain  $\boxed{C}$

