The roots to  $f(f(x)) = 0$  satisfy  $f(x) = r_1$  or  $f(x) = r_2$ , where  $r_1$  and  $r_2$  are the roots of  $f$ . One of these must have exactly one solution, say  $r_1$ . Then the discriminant of the expression   $$ x^2+6x+(c-r_1) $$   is 0. So we must solve the system
\begin{align*}
r_1^2 + 6r_1 + c &= 0 
36 - 4(c-r_1) &= 0.
\end{align*}
This solves to obtain  $c = \frac{11 \pm \sqrt{13}}2$ , of which only  $c = \frac{11-\sqrt{13}}2$  yields real solutions for the  $r_2$  equation.

Suppose  $f(x) = (x-a)(x-b)$ . Then:
\begin{align*}
f(f(x)) = f\left((x-a)(x-b)\right) &= \left((x-a)(x-b)-a\right)\left((x-a)(x-b)-b\right) 
&= \left(x^2 - \left(a+b\right)x + \left(ab-a\right)\right)\left(x^2 - \left(a+b\right)x + \left(ab-b\right)\right) 
&= \left(x^2 - 6x + \left(ab-a\right)\right)\left(x^2 - 6x + \left(ab-b\right)\right).
\end{align*}
Notice, it is impossible for these two products to have a common root as that would imply the products would have another common root which means  $f(f(x))$  has at most two distinct roots. Therefore, one of the products must have the same root. Without the loss of generality, suppose  $ab - a = 9$ . Clearly,  $a + b = -6$ . So:
\begin{align*}
a (b - 1) = a (-7 - a) = 9 \quad \longrightarrow \quad a = \tfrac{-7 \pm \sqrt{13}}{2}, \quad b = \tfrac{-5 \mp \sqrt{13}}{2}.
\end{align*}
Thus, the possible values of  $c$  are the following:
\[
c = 
\begin{cases}
ab = \left(\tfrac{-7 + \sqrt{13}}{2}\right) \cdot \left(\tfrac{-5 - \sqrt{13}}{2}\right) = \tfrac{22 - 2\sqrt{13}}{4} = \tfrac{11 - \sqrt{13}}{2} 
ab = \left(\tfrac{-7 - \sqrt{13}}{2}\right) \cdot \left(\tfrac{-5 + \sqrt{13}}{2}\right) = \tfrac{22 + 2\sqrt{13}}{4} = \tfrac{11 + \sqrt{13}}{2}.
\end{cases}
\]
We can easily confirm that  $c = ab = \boxed{\tfrac{11 - \sqrt{13}}{2}}$  is the only working solution.

