Here's a proof by strong induction on  $|m| + |n|$ .
[rule]
Let  $P(m,n)$  be the given assertion. 
\begin{align*}
&P(x,0) : f(x) \mid f(x) - f(0)  &\implies f(x) \mid f(0) \qquad \qquad (1)
\end{align*}**<span style="color:#f00">Claim: </span>**  $f(n) = f(-n) ~ \forall ~ n \in \mathbb Z$ .**<span style="color:#00f">Proof:</span>** Assume not and pick a  $\alpha$  with  $f(\alpha) > f(-\alpha)$ . 
\begin{align*}
P(0,-\alpha) : f(\alpha) \mid f(0) + f(-\alpha) \implies f(\alpha) \mid f(-\alpha)
\end{align*}
where we used  $f(\alpha) \mid f(0)$  (by  $(1)$ ). This is a contradiction as  $f(\alpha) > f(-\alpha) > 0$ .  $\square$ Now we solve our main problem using strong induction on  $k = |m| + |n|$ . Base cases  $k=0,1$  are direct by  $(1)$  and Claim. Now we do inductive step. If any of  $m,n$  is  $0$ , we are done by  $(1)$ . Assume  $m,n \ne 0$ . Because of Claim we may further assume  $m,n > 0$ . Suppose  $f(m) < f(n)$  (clearly  $m \ne n$ ), to prove  $f(m) \mid f(n)$ . **Case 1:**  $m > n$ . Now  $f(n) \mid f(m) - f(m-n)$ . If  $f(m-n) = f(m)$ , we are done by induction hypothesis. Otherwise  $f(m-n) = f(m) + kf(n)$  with  $k \ge 1$ . But then  $f(n) \le f(m-n)$ , so by induction hypothesis  $f(n) \mid f(m-n)$ . So  $f(n) \mid f(m)$ . But as  $f(n) > f(m)$ , so this is a contradiction.  $\square$ **Case 2:**  $m < n$ . Now  $f(n) \mid f(m) - f(n-m)$ . Write  $f(n-m) = f(m) + kf(n)$  with  $k \ge 0$ . As  $f(n-m) \ge f(m)$ , so by induction hypothesis  $f(m) \mid f(n-m)$ . Now  $f(m) \mid f(n) + f(n-m)$ . Thus  $f(m) \mid f(n)$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$

