*Proof.* We make use of directed angles modulo  $\pi$ . We have  $\measuredangle BMO_X = \measuredangle BMX_b+\measuredangle X_bMO_X = \measuredangle BMX_b+\measuredangle X_bX_cM+\frac{\pi}{2} = \measuredangle BDX_b + \measuredangle X_bX_cD+\measuredangle DX_cM+\frac{\pi}{2} = \measuredangle DX_cX_b + \measuredangle X_bX_cD +\measuredangle DCM+\frac{\pi}{2} = \measuredangle BCM+\frac{\pi}{2} = \measuredangle BMO$  so the claim follows.  $\square$ Claim 1 and Claim 3 imply that  $M$  is one of the intersection point(s) of  $\Gamma,\Gamma_X$ , and Claim 4 implies that it is collinear with their centers, thus  $\Gamma$  and  $\Gamma_X$  are tangent at  $M$ .  $\blacksquare$

