<span style="font-size:175%">Lemma</span>  $AX,BY,CZ$  concur at  $I$ , and  $I$  lies on  $\odot (ABC)$ .

PROOF: Note that  $PB$  and  $RB$  bisect  $\angle YPR$  and  $\angle YRP$ , and so  $B$  is the incenter of  $\triangle YRP$ . This means that  $B$  lies on  $YI$ . Similarly,  $A$  and  $C$  are excenters in  $\triangle XQR$  and  $\triangle ZPQ$ , and so  $A$  and  $C$  lie on  $XI$  and  $ZI$ .

Also, In  $\triangle XQR$ ,  $A=90^{\circ}-\frac{X}{2} \Rightarrow X=180^{\circ}-2A$ . Similarly we get that  $Y=180^{\circ}-2B$  and  $Z=180^{\circ}-2C$ . This means that  $\angle BIC=\angle YIZ=90^{\circ}+\frac{X}{2}=180^{\circ}-A \Rightarrow I$  lies on  $\odot (ABC)$ .

Return to the problem at hand. From Law of Sines, one gets the following with all cyclic variations holding.  $$ \frac{IY}{IZ}=\frac{\sin(\frac{Z}{2})}{\sin(\frac{Y}{2})}=\frac{\cos C}{\cos B}, \text{ } \frac{XY}{XZ}=\frac{\sin Z}{\sin Y}, \text{ } \frac{AB}{AC}=\frac{\sin C}{\sin B}    -(*) $$  And, By Ptolemy on  $ABDC, AB \cdot CD+AC \cdot BD=BC \cdot AD$ . Using third result from  $(*)$  cyclically, we get that  $CD \sin C+BD \sin B=AD \sin A$  - $(**)$ Now,  $\frac{PC}{PD}=\frac{\sin \angle PDC}{\sin \angle PCD}=\frac{\sin \angle DBC}{\sin \angle DCB}=\frac{DC}{DB}$ . 

Also, In  $\triangle BPY, \frac{BY}{BP}=\frac{\sin \frac{\angle DPY}{2}}{\cos B}$  AND In  $\triangle CPZ, \frac{CZ}{CP}=\frac{\sin \frac{\angle DPY}{2}}{\cos C}$  $\Rightarrow \frac{CZ \cos C}{BY \cos B}=\frac{CP}{BP}=\frac{CP^2}{BP \cdot CP}=\frac{PC^2}{PD^2}=\frac{DC^2}{DB^2}$ . 

Similarly, We get that  $\frac{RA}{RD}=\frac{DA}{DB}$  and  $\frac {AX \cos A}{BY \cos B}=\frac{DA^2}{DB^2}$ .

Using  $(**)$  along with the above results, we get that   $$ \sin C\sqrt{CZ \cos C}+\sin B\sqrt{BY \cos B}=\sin A\sqrt{AX \cos A} \Rightarrow \sin 2C\sqrt{\frac{CZ}{\cos C}}+\sin 2B\sqrt{\frac{BY}{\cos B}}=\sin 2A\sqrt{\frac{AX}{\cos A}} \Rightarrow \sin Z\sqrt{\frac{CZ}{\cos C}}+\sin Y\sqrt{\frac{BY}{\cos B}}=\sin X\sqrt{\frac{AX}{\cos A}} $$  Using the first and second results of  $(*)$  in the above equation, we get that  $XY\sqrt{ZC \cdot ZI}+ZX\sqrt{YB \cdot YI}=YZ\sqrt{XA \cdot XI}$ .

Thus, By the [Converse of Casey's Theorem](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c701535h1694131_a_beautiful_theorem_about_tangency_of_circles) on point circles  $\odot (X), \odot (Y), \odot (Z)$  and circle  $\odot (ABC)$ , we get that  $\odot (ABC)$  and  $\odot (XYZ)$  are tangent to each other. Hence, proved.

