Let  $P$  be the point on  $\Gamma$  that  $\ell$  is tangent at. We see that the reflection of  $P$  in  $AB$  lies on  $(AHB)$  and so on where  $H$  is the orthocenter of  $ABC$ . Let  $P_a$  be the reflection of  $P$  in  $BC$ , and define  $P_b,P_c$  similarly. Then,  $\ell_a$  is simply the tangent to  $(HBC)$  at  $P_a$ . Note that a homothety  $h$  with scale factor  $1/2$  at  $P$  sends  $P_a,P_b,P_c$  to the feet from  $P$  to the sides of the triangle, and by a well known lemma of simson lines, we have that  $h(P_a)h(P_b)h(P_c)$  and the midpoint of  $PH$  are collinear. Applying  $h^{-1}$ , we learn that  $P_aP_bP_cH$  are collinear. In fact, the following problem statement is now true for any point  $H$ :

Let  $H$  be a point, and let  $P_a\in(HBC)$  and so on with  $P_aP_bP_cH$  collinear. Then defining  $\ell_a$  etc in the same way, and defining  $D=\ell_b\cap\ell_c$  etc, we have  $(DEF)$  is tangent to  $(ABC)$ .

The first step is to invert about  $H$ . We get figure 2. Note that  $(HEF)$  is tangent to  $BC$  at  $P_a$  and cyclic variations (we drop all primes). We claim that  $G$ , the miquel point of  $ABP_aP_b$  is the desired tangency point. By Miquel point stuff, clearly  $G\in(ABC)$ . To aid in the proof, we have a lemma.

<u>Lemma:</u>  $P_aP_bCF$  and cyclic variations are cyclic quadrilaterals.

<u>Proof of Lemma:</u> This is just directed angles. We see that
\begin{align*}
\angle P_bFP_a &= \angle P_bFH+\angle HFP_a 
&= \angle CP_bP_a + \angle P_bP_aC 
&= \angle P_aCP_b,
\end{align*}
as desired.  $\blacksquare$ We will now show that  $G\in(DEF)$ . This is also just directed angles. We have
\begin{align*}
\angle DGE &= \angle DGP_b+\angle P_bGP_a+\angle P_aGE 
&= \angle DP_cP_b + \angle P_bCP_a + \angle P_aP_cE 
&= \angle DP_cE+\angle ACB 
&= \angle DHE+\angle ACB,
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
\angle DFE &= \angle DFH + \angle HFE 
&= \angle DP_bH + \angle HP_aE 
&= (\angle DP_bA-\angle HP_bC)+(\angle HP_aC-\angle EP_aB) 
&= \angle ACB + \angle DP_bA+\angle BP_aE 
&= \angle ACB + \angle DP_cA + \angle BP_cE 
&= \angle ACB + \angle DHP_c + \angle P_cHE 
&= \angle ACB + \angle DHE,
\end{align*}
so  $\angle DGE=\angle DHE$ , so  $G\in(DEF)$ . It remains to show the tangency, which one can see is equivalent to showing
\[\angle DFG-\angle ACG = \angle DGA.\]
Note that  $\angle DGA=\angle DP_cA=\angle DHP_c$ . We also have
\[\angle DFG=\angle DFP_b+\angle P_bFG=\angle DHP_b+\angle P_bCG=\angle DGA+\angle ACG,\]
so  $\angle DFG-\angle ACG = \angle DGA$ , so in fact  $(DEFG)$  and  $(ABCG)$  are tangent, as desired.

Let  $\ell$  touch  $\Gamma$  at  $X$ . Make the following definitions with cyclic variations defined similarly as well: Denote  $A_1=\ell_b\cap\ell_c$  and  $A'=\ell\cap\ell_a$ . Let  $X_A$  be the reflection of  $X$  over  $\overline{BC}$  and  $Y_A=\overline{BX_C}\cap\overline{CX_B}$ .
