We use barycentric coordinates with respect to  $ABC$ . To begin, we have  $D=(0:S_C:S_B)$  and  $G=(1:1:1)$ , so the equation of  $\overline{GD}$  is given by  $$ 0=\text{det}\left( \begin{array}{ccc} 0 & S_C & S_B  1 & 1 & 1  x & y & z \end{array} \right)=(S_C-S_B)x+S_By-S_Cz. $$  Intersecting this with the lines  $y=0$  and  $z=0$  respectively yields  $P=(S_C:0:S_C-S_B)$  and  $Q=(S_B:S_B-S_C:0)$ , which combined with  $B_0=(1:1:0)$  and  $C_0=(1:0:1)$  gives  $$ (Y,B_1;A,B)\stackrel{X'}{=}(P,B_0;A,B)=(A,C_0;Q,C)\stackrel{X'}{=}(A,C_1;Y,C). $$  Since  $BAYC$  is an isosceles trapezoid, it follows that  $\overline{B_1C_1}\parallel\overline{AY}\parallel\overline{B_0C_0}$ . Thus,  $(B_0XC_0)$  is tangent to  $(ABC)$  by homothety, so  $X=X'$ .

