<blockquote>Let  $ABC$  be an acute triangle with circumcircle  $\Omega$ . Let  $B_0$  be the midpoint of  $AC$  and let  $C_0$  be the midpoint of  $AB$ . Let  $D$  be the foot of the altitude from  $A$  and let  $G$  be the centroid of the triangle  $ABC$ . Let  $\omega$  be a circle through  $B_0$  and  $C_0$  that is tangent to the circle  $\Omega$  at a point  $X\not= A$ . Prove that the points  $D,G$  and  $X$  are collinear.
</blockquote>
Let  $B_o, C_o$  meet  $\Omega$  again at  $M,N$ , respectively. Let  $L, A_o$  be the midpoints of  $MN, BC$  respectively and let  $P=MN \cap AA$ , where  $AA$  is the tangent to  $\Omega$ . Also, redefine  $X$  as the intersection of  $DG$  and  $\Omega$  that lies in the arc  $BC$  not containing  $A$ . Finally, set  $T$  to be a point on  $\Omega$  such that  $AT \parallel BC$ .

Now, consider the homothety  $\mathcal{H}(G,-2)$ . Clearly,  $A_oL \perp BC$  and so  $\mathcal{H}: L \mapsto D$ . Also,  $\mathcal{H}:D \mapsto T$ . Hence,  $T, L$  lie on  $DG$ . Hence,   $$ -1=(MN,L\infty) \overset{T}{=} (MN, XA) $$  Thus,  $PX$  is tangent to  $\Omega$ . Clearly since  $AC_oB_o$  is tangent to  $\Omega$ , hence we get  $PX^2=PA^2=PB_o \cdot PC_o$  and so  $\Omega, (XB_oC_o)$  are tangent.  $\blacksquare$ **Fun Fact**: This point  $X$  is the point  $Q$  from [All-Russian olympiad 2018 10.2](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/q3h1632766p10410510). Interestingly enough this problem is proposed by a Russian and the problem that I linked to is from Russia's national olympiad!

