Note that  $\angle AIE=\frac{A}{2}+\frac{B}{2}=\overarc{DCE}=180-\overarc{ABD}-\overarc{EC}=\angle AFE$ , so AIFE, and similarly BIGD, is cyclic.

Let the second intersection of  $(AIFE)$  and  $(BIGD)$  be  $J$ . If  $J=I$ , define  $IJ$  to be the radical axis of the two circles.

Essentially, it is sufficient to prove that  $K$  and  $P$  lie on  $IJ$  because radical axis theorem states that  $IJ$ ,  $BD$ ,  $AE$  are either parallel or concurrent.

If  $I=J$ ,  $AEDB$  must be an isosceles trapezoid, because the four points lie on a circle and make two tangent circles. This means  $C=\frac{A}{2}+\frac{B}{2}$ . This means that  $\angle AKB=180-2C=180-A-B=180-\angle AJB$  so AJBK is cyclic. Then  $KJB=KAB=C=EBD$ , so  $K$  lies on  $IJ$ .

If  $I\neq J$ ,  $\angle AJB=\angle AJI+\angle IJB=\angle AEI+\angle BDI=2C=180-AKB$ , so AKBJ is cyclic. Then, since  $\angle AJI=\angle BJI$  and  $\overarc{AK}=\overarc{BK}$ ,  $K$  lies on  $IJ$ .

Now,  $\angle FJG=360-\angle FJI-\angle JGI=\angle FEI+\angle GEI=180-\angle DIE=180-\angle FPG$  so  $FJGP$  is cyclic. Then,  $\angle IPF=\angle KIA=\angle JID=\angle JGF=\angle JPF$ , so  $P$  lies on  $IJ$  as well, so we're done. (note the above angle chasing works even if  $I=J$ , we just use  $\angle IJX$ =the angle between IJ and IX)

