By Brokard's Theorem on  $ABDE,$  it suffices to show that  $K,I,P$  are collinear where  $I$  is the incenter of  $\triangle ABC.$  Observe that since  $B\in(AED)$  and  $\measuredangle GBI=\measuredangle EBA,$  we find that  $B$  is the spiral center sending  $IG$  to  $AE$  and thus it must lie on  $(IDG);$  similarly,  $A$  must also lie on  $(IEF).$  Now let  $X=PG\cap (BIDG)$  and  $Y=PF\cap (AIEF).$  Since  $BX=IG=AY,$  we find that  $\frac{KA}{AY}=\frac{KB}{KX}.$  Observe that
\begin{align*}
\measuredangle GXB&=\measuredangle GBI+\measuredangle IDB 
&=\measuredangle GBI+\measuredangle DIB+\measuredangle DIB 
&=\measuredangle GBI +\measuredangle AIB+\measuredangle AIB 
&=\measuredangle GBI + \measuredangle ACB 
&=\measuredangle GBI + \measuredangle ABK 
&=\measuredangle IBK,
\end{align*}
whence  $K,B,X$  are collinear, and the same argument gives  $K,A,Y$  collinear. Thus,  $\triangle KAB\stackrel{+}{\sim}\triangle KYX$  by SAS and we find that  $ABXY$  is an isosceles trapezoid, meaning that  $KI$  is the radical axis of  $(BIDG)$  and  $(AIEF).$  Furthermore, it follows from  $PF||AD$  and  $PG||BE$  that  $\measuredangle YIE=\measuredangle YFE=\measuredangle ADE=\measuredangle IXG=\measuredangle XIB,$  so  $X,I,Y$  are collinear and  $\measuredangle GFY=\measuredangle GDI=\measuredangle GXY,$  implying that  $XYFG$  is cyclic; hence,  $P,$  the radical center of  $(XYFG),(BIDG),(AIEF),$  lies on  $KI$  as desired.  $\blacksquare$ [asy] /* Geogebra to Asymptote conversion, documentation at artofproblemsolving.com/Wiki go to User:Azjps/geogebra */
