We begin with a claim.

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim:**</span> The radical axis of  $\odot(BID)$  and  $\odot(AIE)$  is the line  $\overline{PI}$ *Proof:* Firstly notice that   $$ \angle PGC=\angle EBC=\angle EBA=\angle EDA=\angle PFG $$   hence we get that  $\odot(PFG)$  is tangent to  $\overline{CG}$  and similiarly to  $\overline{CF}$ . Now notice that clearly   $$ \angle BGD=\angle CGF=180^\circ-\angle FPG=180^\circ-\angle AIB=\angle BID $$   and hence  $G \in \odot(BID)$ . Similiarly we get that  $F \in \odot(AIE)$ . Now define  $H=\odot(BID) \cap \odot(AIE)$  such that  $H \neq I$ . Notice that   $$ \angle GHF=360^\circ-(\angle IHG+\angle IHF)=360^\circ-(360^\circ-\angle CAI-\angle CBI)=90^\circ-\angle ACI=180^\circ-\angle FPG $$    and hence  $H \in \odot(FPG)$ . To finish note that   $$ \angle PHG=\angle CGP=\angle IBG $$   and this proves the claim  $\qquad \square$ .

Now back to the main problem. Notice that   $$ \angle AHB=360^\circ-(\angle AHF+\angle BHG+\angle FHG)=\angle AEF+\angle BDG-\angle FHG=2 \angle C $$   and hence  $H \in \odot(AKB)$ . Now just notice that   $$ \angle KHB=\angle ACB=\angle ADB=\angle IHB $$   and hence we have that  $K,I,P$  are collinear . Now by radical axis theorem onto  $\odot(AIE),\odot(BID),\odot(ABC)$  we are done  $\qquad \blacksquare$

