First, I prove the following lemma: **<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>**  $\overline{MD}, \overline{ME},$  and  $\overline{TA}$  are tangent to  $(AED).$  
*Proof.* First, I show that  $\overline{TA}$  is tangent to  $(AED).$  Let  $H$  denote the orthocenter of  $\overline{ABC}$  and let  $F$  denote the foot of the perpendicular from  $A$  to  $\overline{BC}.$  

Angle-chasing, we get
\begin{align*}
    \angle{TAC} &= \angle{ACB} 
    &= 180^\circ-\angle{EHF} 
    &= \angle{EHA},
\end{align*}
implying that  $\overline{TA}$  is tangent to  $(AED),$  as desired. 

Now, I prove that  $\overline{MD}$  is tangent to  $(AED)$  and then  $\overline{ME}$  will follow by symmetry. 

Angle-chasing once again, we get
\begin{align*}
    \angle{MDE} &= \angle{EDC}+\angle{CDM} 
    &= \angle{EAF}+\angle{MCD} 
    &= (90^\circ-\angle{ACB})+(90^\circ-\angle{ABC}) 
    &= 180^\circ-\angle{ABC}-\angle{ACB} 
    &= \angle{BAC},
\end{align*}
implying that  $\overline{MD}$  is tangent to  $(AED),$  as desired, concluding the proof of this lemma.  $\blacksquare$  

Now, since the radical axis of two circles passes through the midpoints of both of their common tangents and  $K$  and  $L$  are the midpoints of  $\overline{ME}$  and  $\overline{MD},$  we know that the radical axis of  $(AED)$  and the circle of zero radius centered at  $M$  is line  $KL.$  Therefore, since  $T$  lies on line  $KL,$  its power with respect to the two circles must be the same, i.e.   $$ TA^2=TM^2 \implies TA=TM, $$   as desired.  $\square$

