<details><summary>Solution - grinding Complex Numbers</summary>$$ \color{red} \spadesuit\color{red} \boxed{\textbf{Setup.}}\color{red} \spadesuit $$  Consider  $\odot ABC$  as the unit circle,  $A=a, B=b, C=c$ .  $$ \color{blue} \clubsuit\color{blue} \boxed{\textbf{Circle = bad.}}\color{blue} \clubsuit $$  We want to get rid of the second circle; motivated by this, we simply consider that it implies  $BD=BA=BE$ . Thus, we define  $X$  as the reflection of  $B$  across  $AE$  and  $Y$  as the reflection of  $B$  across  $AD$ . Thus, we know that  $XEBA$  and  $ABDY$  are parallelograms, and from the reflection formula (reflecting  $B$  across the midpoints of  $AD, AE$ ), we get that  $$ X=a+c-\frac{ac}{b}; \; Y=a+a-\frac{a^2}{b}. $$  Notice that the parallelogram condition gives us that  $X+B=A+E$  and  $A+D=B+Y$ ; thus, knowing the other three we may obtain  $e$  and  $d$ :  $$ e=x+b-a=c-\frac{ac}{b}+b; d=b+y-a=a+b-\frac{a^2}{b}. $$  We have now successfully removed the circle...  $$ \color{magenta} \bigstar\color{magenta} \boxed{\textbf{Finishing touches.}}\color{magenta} \bigstar $$  ...and all that remains is to prove that  $$ \frac{h-e}{h-d}=\frac{\overline{h-e}}{\overline{h-d}}. $$  Knowing that the orthocenter is  $H$  and that  $h=a+b+c$ , we have  $$ \begin{aligned}
\frac{h-e}{h-d} &=  \frac{\frac{-ab-ac}{b}}{\frac{-a^2-bc}{b}}
&=\frac{ab+ac}{a^2+bc}.
\end{aligned} $$  and  $$ \begin{aligned}
\overline{\left(\frac{e-h}{d-h}\right)} &= \frac{\tfrac{1}{ab}+\tfrac{1}{ac}}{\tfrac{1}{bc}+\tfrac{1}{a^2}} 
&= \frac{ac+ab}{a^2+bc},
\end{aligned}  $$  thus, we are done.</details>

